  "lean_proofs": "/-\n# The Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems\n\nThis file proves the **Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems** for the catalog's\nsynthetic-HoTT layer, completing the program announced in\n`Catalog/Logic/HoTT/Foundations.lean`. There, the docstring of `IdentitySystem`\nstates:\n\n  \"The fundamental theorem says this data yields an equivalence\n   `(a₀ = a) ≃' R a` for all `a`.\"\n\nbut only the *definitions* were provided. Here we supply the proof, together with\nseveral structural corollaries (transport of contractibility along an\nequivalence, contractibility of the base fibre, and uniqueness of identity\nsystems = \"homotopy-initiality\").\n\n## Relationship to catalog\n- Builds directly on `HoTTFound.Contractible`, `HoTTFound.Equiv'`,\n  `HoTTFound.IdentitySystem`, `HoTTFound.pathIdentitySystem`.\n- `fundamentalIdentitySystem` is the central theorem promised — but not proved —\n  in `Foundations.lean`.\n- `Equiv'.contractible` extends the `Equiv'` API of the catalog.\n- `idSys_unique` is the \"homotopy-initial family\" statement: identity systems\n  based at a point are unique up to fibrewise equivalence.\n-/\n\nimport Mathlib\nimport Catalog.Logic.HoTT.Foundations\n\nuniverse u v w\n\nnamespace HoTTFound\n\n/-! ## Transport of contractibility along equivalences -/\n\nnamespace Equiv'\n\n/-- An equivalence transports a contractibility witness from its domain to its\n    codomain. This is the data-level statement that `Contractible` is invariant\n    under `≃'`. -/\ndef contractible {α : Sort u} {β : Sort v}\n    (e : α ≃' β) (h : Contractible α) : Contractible β where\n  center := e.toFun h.center\n  contr := fun y => by\n    rw [← e.right_inv y]\n    exact congrArg e.toFun (h.contr (e.invFun y))\n\nend Equiv'\n\n/-! ## Encode / decode for an identity system -/\n\nvariable {A : Sort u} {a₀ : A} {R : A → Sort v}\n\n/-- The canonical \"encode\" map of an identity system: transport the reflexivity\n    witness along a path. -/\ndef idSysEncode (S : IdentitySystem A a₀ R) (a : A) : (a₀ = a) → R a :=\n  fun p => p ▸ S.rflR\n\n/-- The canonical \"decode\" map of an identity system: extract a path from the\n    contractibility of the total space `Σ' a, R a`. -/\ndef idSysDecode (S : IdentitySystem A a₀ R) (a : A) : R a → (a₀ = a) :=\n  fun r =>\n    have eq : (⟨a, r⟩ : Σ' x, R x) = ⟨a₀, S.rflR⟩ :=\n      (S.contr_total.contr ⟨a, r⟩).trans S.center_eq\n    (congrArg PSigma.fst eq).symm\n\n/-! ## The Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems -/\n\n/-- **The Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems.**\n\n    For any identity system `S` on `A` based at `a₀` with family `R`, the encode\n    map `(a₀ = a) → R a` is an equivalence, with inverse `idSysDecode`. This is\n    the central result promised in `Foundations.lean`: an identity system makes\n    `R` behave exactly like the based path family of `a₀`. -/\ndef fundamentalIdentitySystem (S : IdentitySystem A a₀ R) (a : A) :\n    (a₀ = a) ≃' R a where\n  toFun := idSysEncode S a\n  invFun := idSysDecode S a\n  left_inv := fun p => proof_irrel _ _\n  right_inv := fun r => by\n    have eq : (⟨a, r⟩ : Σ' x, R x) = ⟨a₀, S.rflR⟩ :=\n      (S.contr_total.contr ⟨a, r⟩).trans S.center_eq\n    rw [PSigma.mk.injEq] at eq\n    obtain ⟨hb, hf⟩ := eq\n    unfold idSysEncode idSysDecode\n    subst hb\n    simp_all\n\n/-! ## Corollaries -/\n\n/-- In any identity system, the base fibre `R a₀` is contractible. This is the\n    data-level shadow of \"an identity system at `a₀` has a contractible value at\n    `a₀`\", obtained by transporting contractibility of `a₀ = a₀` across the\n    fundamental equivalence. -/\ndef idSys_base_fiber_contractible (S : IdentitySystem A a₀ R) :\n    Contractible (R a₀) :=\n  Equiv'.contractible (fundamentalIdentitySystem S a₀)\n    ⟨rfl, fun _ => proof_irrel _ _⟩\n\n/-- **Homotopy-initiality / uniqueness of identity systems.**\n\n    Any two identity systems based at the same point `a₀` (with possibly\n    different families `R` and `R'`) are *fibrewise equivalent*: `R a ≃' R' a`\n    for every `a`. Identity systems based at `a₀` are therefore unique up to\n    equivalence, which is the precise sense in which the based path family is\n    homotopy-initial. -/\ndef idSys_unique {R' : A → Sort w}\n    (S : IdentitySystem A a₀ R) (S' : IdentitySystem A a₀ R') (a : A) :\n    R a ≃' R' a :=\n  (fundamentalIdentitySystem S a).symm.trans (fundamentalIdentitySystem S' a)\n\n/-- Sanity check connecting to `pathIdentitySystem` from `Foundations.lean`:\n    for the based path family `R a := (a₀ = a)`, the fundamental equivalence at\n    `a₀` sends `rfl` to `rfl`, i.e. its encode map is the canonical one. -/\ntheorem fundamental_path_encode_rfl (a₀ : A) :\n    (fundamentalIdentitySystem (pathIdentitySystem a₀) a₀).toFun rfl = rfl :=\n  rfl\n\nend HoTTFound\n",
  "future_directions": "# Future Directions — The Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems and Homotopy-Initial Families\n\n## Synthesis of this cycle\n\nThe catalog's synthetic-HoTT layer (Catalog/Logic/HoTT/Foundations.lean) introduced\ndata-carrying `Contractible`, a bespoke `Equiv'` with full computational content, and the\n`IdentitySystem` structure — an `A`-indexed family `R` equipped with a reflexivity witness\nand a *correctly-centred contractible total space* `Σ' a, R a`. Crucially, the file *stated*\nin its docstring that \"the fundamental theorem says this data yields an equivalence\n`(a₀ = a) ≃' R a`\", but it never proved it. That promissory note was the conceptual hole in\nthe layer.\n\nThis cycle closes it. Catalog/Logic/HoTT/IdentitySystems.lean proves the\n**Fundamental Theorem of Identity Systems** (`fundamentalIdentitySystem`): for any\n`IdentitySystem A a₀ R` and any `a : A`, encode/decode are mutually inverse, so\n`(a₀ = a) ≃' R a`. The forward map is path transport of the reflexivity witness; the inverse\nis recovered from contractibility of the total space. We then harvest three structural\ncorollaries:\n\n- `Equiv'.contractible` — contractibility is an invariant of `≃'` (a missing piece of the\n  catalog's `Equiv'` API);\n- `idSys_base_fiber_contractible` — in any identity system the base fibre `R a₀` is\n  contractible;\n- `idSys_unique` — **homotopy-initiality**: any two identity systems based at the same point\n  are *fibrewise equivalent*, so the based path family is unique up to equivalence.\n\nAll results are `sorry`-free and depend only on `propext`.\n\n## Results summary\n\n| Theorem | Statement | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fundamentalIdentitySystem` | `IdentitySystem A a₀ R → (a₀ = a) ≃' R a` | proved |\n| `Equiv'.contractible` | `α ≃' β → Contractible α → Contractible β` | proved |\n| `idSys_base_fiber_contractible` | `IdentitySystem A a₀ R → Contractible (R a₀)` | proved |\n| `idSys_unique` | two identity systems at `a₀` ⇒ `R a ≃' R' a` | proved |\n| `fundamental_path_encode_rfl` | encode of the path family sends `rfl ↦ rfl` | proved |\n\nThe decisive structural fact exploited throughout: in Lean 4 `Eq` is `Prop`-valued, so the\npath side of every equivalence is automatically a subsingleton (UIP). This made one triangle\nof the fundamental equivalence free and concentrated all homotopical content into transporting\na fibre witness back along a recovered base path.\n\n## Research directions\n\n### 1. The converse: contractible total space *characterizes* identity systems\n\nWe proved that an identity system yields a fibrewise equivalence to the path family. The\nsharper, fully bidirectional statement is the genuine fundamental theorem: a family `R` with\n`r₀ : R a₀` is an identity system **iff** the canonical map `(a₀ = a) → R a` is an equivalence\nfor every `a`, **iff** the total space `Σ' a, R a` is contractible. We have one of the three\nimplications; the conjecture is that the remaining two are provable inside the catalog's\ndata-carrying `Contractible`/`Equiv'` setting.\n\n### 2. Higher coherence in a univalent setting\n\nRe-examine the construction where the path side is not 0-truncated, recovering both nontrivial\ncoherence triangles and the full homotopical content.\n\n### 3. Library consolidation\n\nPromote `Equiv'.contractible` and the `PSigma.mk.injEq`/`subst`/`eq_of_heq` transport pattern\nto reusable lemmas of the synthetic-homotopy layer.\n",
